1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,544
Alkaff Mansion and the Alkaffs:

2
00:00:02,624 --> 00:00:05,925
A Hadhrami Family on Telok Blangah Hill

3
00:00:05,925 --> 00:00:10,501
On top of Telok Blangah Hill, above
Keppel Harbour, stands a white

4
00:00:10,581 --> 00:00:15,025
two-storey house with a red-tiled
roof and a small clock tower.

5
00:00:15,025 --> 00:00:19,859
It was built around 1918 as a
weekend retreat by the Alkaffs,

6
00:00:19,939 --> 00:00:25,900
an Arab family whose fortune began
with trade in spices, sugar and coffee.

7
00:00:25,900 --> 00:00:29,215
The family came from the
Hadhramaut, a long desert

8
00:00:29,295 --> 00:00:32,814
valley in what is now Yemen,
and for a few decades it

9
00:00:32,894 --> 00:00:36,425
owned some of the best-known
landmarks in Singapore.

10
00:00:36,425 --> 00:00:40,649
The Alkaffs were sayyids, a
family that traces its descent to

11
00:00:40,729 --> 00:00:45,175
the Prophet Muhammad, from the
town of Tarim in the Hadhramaut.

12
00:00:45,175 --> 00:00:48,162
For centuries, men from
the valley had sailed

13
00:00:48,242 --> 00:00:51,296
out to the ports of the
Indian Ocean to trade,

14
00:00:51,376 --> 00:00:54,375
and many of them settled
in the Malay world.

15
00:00:54,375 --> 00:00:57,450
According to the National Heritage Board,

16
00:00:57,530 --> 00:01:02,605
the founder of the family firm,
Syed Shaik bin Abdul Rahman Alkaff,

17
00:01:02,685 --> 00:01:06,225
came to Singapore from
the Hadhramaut in 1852.

18
00:01:06,225 --> 00:01:10,151
His firm, Alkaff and Company,
traded in spices, sugar,

19
00:01:10,231 --> 00:01:14,602
coffee and textiles between
India and the Dutch East Indies,

20
00:01:14,682 --> 00:01:17,650
and it put much of its profit into land.

21
00:01:17,650 --> 00:01:21,441
By the turn of the 20th
century the Alkaffs were among

22
00:01:21,521 --> 00:01:24,380
the largest property owners in Singapore,

23
00:01:24,460 --> 00:01:29,550
alongside two other Hadhrami families,
the Alsagoffs and the Aljunieds.

24
00:01:29,550 --> 00:01:33,650
I wrote about one of the
Alsagoffs in an earlier post.

25
00:01:33,650 --> 00:01:36,625
Like many Hadhrami
merchants, the family kept

26
00:01:36,705 --> 00:01:39,612
close ties with home,
and a good deal of the

27
00:01:39,692 --> 00:01:42,950
money made in Singapore
went back to the valley.

28
00:01:42,950 --> 00:01:47,640
In Tarim, the Alkaffs built a
cluster of large mud-brick palaces,

29
00:01:47,720 --> 00:01:50,875
which are still the
town's grandest houses.

30
00:01:50,875 --> 00:01:55,015
One of the family, Sayyid
Abubakr bin Shaikh Alkaff,

31
00:01:55,095 --> 00:02:00,775
was born in Singapore around 1890
and later settled in the Hadhramaut.

32
00:02:00,775 --> 00:02:04,236
He paid for a school and
a hospital in Tarim and

33
00:02:04,316 --> 00:02:08,225
helped finance a road from
the interior to the coast.

34
00:02:08,225 --> 00:02:12,995
In 1936 he helped the British
official Harold Ingrams broker a

35
00:02:13,075 --> 00:02:17,925
truce between the valley's warring
Qu'aiti and Kathiri states.

36
00:02:17,925 --> 00:02:23,065
The family's best-known building
in town was the Alkaff Arcade,

37
00:02:23,145 --> 00:02:27,950
which opened on Collyer Quay
on the 27th of November 1909.

38
00:02:27,950 --> 00:02:32,558
It was designed by the architect
David McLeod Craik of Swan and

39
00:02:32,638 --> 00:02:37,475
Maclaren in a Moorish style, with
two onion domes at the corners.

40
00:02:37,475 --> 00:02:42,732
Inside, a covered passage ran from
Raffles Square through to the waterfront,

41
00:02:42,812 --> 00:02:46,675
lined with shops on the ground
floor and offices above.

42
00:02:46,675 --> 00:02:50,299
The Straits Times called it
"possibly the most unique"

43
00:02:50,379 --> 00:02:53,316
of the city's new
buildings, and it is often

44
00:02:53,396 --> 00:02:57,100
described as Singapore's
first indoor shopping centre.

45
00:02:57,100 --> 00:03:00,550
The Arcade replaced the
old office and godowns

46
00:03:00,630 --> 00:03:03,700
of the trading firm Guthrie and Company.

47
00:03:03,700 --> 00:03:07,117
The first tenants included
the shipping line P&O

48
00:03:07,197 --> 00:03:09,521
and the oil company Standard Oil,

49
00:03:09,601 --> 00:03:13,900
and Alkaff and Company kept
its own office in the building.

50
00:03:13,900 --> 00:03:17,530
At the opening luncheon,
according to the Straits Times,

51
00:03:17,610 --> 00:03:21,041
the chronicler Charles Burton
Buckley gave the speech

52
00:03:21,121 --> 00:03:24,500
on behalf of Syed Shaik,
who did not speak English.

53
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:28,397
The family also owned, for a
time, the Hotel de l'Europe,

54
00:03:28,477 --> 00:03:31,396
one of the city's grand
hotels, on the site

55
00:03:31,476 --> 00:03:34,825
where the old Supreme
Court building now stands.

56
00:03:34,825 --> 00:03:40,617
Around 1930, the head of the family,
Syed Abdul Rahman bin Shaikh Alkaff,

57
00:03:40,697 --> 00:03:44,800
opened a park on his land
off Upper Serangoon Road.

58
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:47,808
He brought in a Japanese
landscape gardener,

59
00:03:47,888 --> 00:03:51,386
and the result, the Alkaff
Lake Gardens, is said to

60
00:03:51,466 --> 00:03:54,975
have been Singapore's first
Japanese-style garden.

61
00:03:54,975 --> 00:03:58,277
It had a man-made lake
with boats and fishing,

62
00:03:58,357 --> 00:04:03,202
artificial hills, Japanese bridges
and tea houses, and in the 1930s

63
00:04:03,282 --> 00:04:07,325
it was a popular place for
an evening or a weekend out.

64
00:04:07,325 --> 00:04:10,900
Nearby, on his land, he paid for a mosque.

65
00:04:10,900 --> 00:04:14,591
The Alkaff Upper Serangoon
Mosque opened on the

66
00:04:14,671 --> 00:04:19,004
24th of June 1932, with a
slender Ottoman-style minaret

67
00:04:19,084 --> 00:04:22,775
and a prayer hall of
Moorish horseshoe arches.

68
00:04:22,775 --> 00:04:26,825
It was made a national monument in 2014.

69
00:04:26,825 --> 00:04:32,348
That same year, 1932, another member
of the family, Syed Ahmad Alkaff,

70
00:04:32,428 --> 00:04:37,150
helped pay for a mosque at
Kampung Melayu, near Kaki Bukit.

71
00:04:37,150 --> 00:04:42,400
It was rebuilt in the 1990s and
still carries the family name.

72
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:46,884
The hill above Telok Blangah was
then known as Mount Washington,

73
00:04:46,964 --> 00:04:49,593
possibly because of the family's close

74
00:04:49,673 --> 00:04:53,025
ties with Singapore's
small American community.

75
00:04:53,025 --> 00:04:57,058
A member of the family
bought the land in 1916,

76
00:04:57,138 --> 00:05:00,550
and the house was finished around 1918.

77
00:05:00,550 --> 00:05:05,520
In 1989 the Straits Times credited
it to Syed Abdulrahman Alkaff,

78
00:05:05,600 --> 00:05:08,473
who was born in the Hadhramaut in 1867

79
00:05:08,553 --> 00:05:12,124
and came to Singapore
late in the 19th century,

80
00:05:12,204 --> 00:05:16,400
though some accounts name
other members of the family.

81
00:05:16,400 --> 00:05:19,030
It was a weekend house, not a home,

82
00:05:19,110 --> 00:05:21,975
and the Alkaffs used it to entertain.

83
00:05:21,975 --> 00:05:25,741
Through the 1920s and 1930s the newspapers

84
00:05:25,821 --> 00:05:29,300
reported receptions and parties there.

85
00:05:29,300 --> 00:05:33,622
In 1930 a group of young
Arabs hosted Malay footballers

86
00:05:33,702 --> 00:05:37,783
at the house, and in 1936
the Chinese Consul-General

87
00:05:37,863 --> 00:05:42,425
held a reception there for a
visiting general from China.

88
00:05:42,425 --> 00:05:45,377
In December 1941, as war reached Malaya,

89
00:05:45,457 --> 00:05:49,547
the British military took
over the Alkaff Lake Gardens,

90
00:05:49,627 --> 00:05:54,100
and a volunteer ambulance unit
made its headquarters there.

91
00:05:54,100 --> 00:05:57,312
A large Red Cross sign
was laid out on the grass,

92
00:05:57,392 --> 00:06:01,411
but after the unit left, the
gardens were bombed and shelled,

93
00:06:01,491 --> 00:06:05,925
and during the Japanese occupation
they were closed to the public.

94
00:06:05,925 --> 00:06:10,725
Syed Abdul Rahman himself
was kept out of his own park.

95
00:06:10,725 --> 00:06:14,450
After the war, the
family's fortunes faded.

96
00:06:14,450 --> 00:06:19,981
Rent control, which from 1947 held
many rents at their pre-war levels,

97
00:06:20,061 --> 00:06:24,550
cut into the income of landlords
like the Arab families.

98
00:06:24,550 --> 00:06:28,120
Syed Abdul Rahman bin Shaikh Alkaff

99
00:06:28,200 --> 00:06:31,225
died in Tarim in August 1948.

100
00:06:31,225 --> 00:06:36,565
In July 1950 the gardens were sold
to the Sennett Realty Company,

101
00:06:36,645 --> 00:06:40,317
which planned a housing
estate of about 1,400

102
00:06:40,397 --> 00:06:44,900
houses for some 10,000 people
on the land around them.

103
00:06:44,900 --> 00:06:48,922
The Straits Times reported that
the lake would be filled in,

104
00:06:49,002 --> 00:06:52,625
and readers wrote in asking
for the park to be saved.

105
00:06:52,625 --> 00:06:55,814
The lake survived until 1964, when it was

106
00:06:55,894 --> 00:06:59,800
drained and the land was
cleared for new schools.

107
00:06:59,800 --> 00:07:03,900
Cedar Girls' Secondary School
stands on the site today.

108
00:07:03,900 --> 00:07:08,844
The Arcade was sold in 1973
and pulled down in March 1978

109
00:07:08,924 --> 00:07:14,125
to make way for the office
tower that now carries its name.

110
00:07:14,125 --> 00:07:18,227
The house on Mount Washington
stood empty for years

111
00:07:18,307 --> 00:07:21,917
after the war, and a
Buddhist organisation is

112
00:07:21,997 --> 00:07:26,425
reported to have used it in
the 1970s and early 1980s.

113
00:07:26,425 --> 00:07:30,223
In the late 1980s, the
house was restored as part

114
00:07:30,303 --> 00:07:34,021
of a plan to make the
hill a tourist attraction,

115
00:07:34,101 --> 00:07:37,900
and in September 1990 it
opened as a restaurant.

116
00:07:37,900 --> 00:07:42,551
Its interior was fitted out in
the style of a rich Singapore

117
00:07:42,631 --> 00:07:47,833
home of the 1920s and 1930s, and
its speciality was the rijsttafel,

118
00:07:47,913 --> 00:07:52,250
the Dutch-Indonesian "rice
table" of many small dishes.

119
00:07:52,250 --> 00:07:55,790
At the sound of a gong, a
line of women in kebayas

120
00:07:55,870 --> 00:07:59,200
came out of the kitchen,
each carrying a dish.

121
00:07:59,200 --> 00:08:02,123
When I was working at Security Pacific,

122
00:08:02,203 --> 00:08:04,975
I went to a lunch at Alkaff Mansion.

123
00:08:04,975 --> 00:08:07,581
It was a slightly more expensive place,

124
00:08:07,661 --> 00:08:11,725
and brokers loved a chance to
spend money on their clients.

125
00:08:11,725 --> 00:08:14,512
It was cooler up there than down in town,

126
00:08:14,592 --> 00:08:16,900
because the house sits higher up.

127
00:08:16,900 --> 00:08:19,976
I have wanted to go back,
but it is out of the

128
00:08:20,056 --> 00:08:23,075
way now that I no longer
drive in Singapore.

129
00:08:23,075 --> 00:08:25,399
The restaurant closed in 2003,

130
00:08:25,479 --> 00:08:29,325
and the house stood empty
again for eight years.

131
00:08:29,325 --> 00:08:33,240
It was given conservation status in 2005,

132
00:08:33,320 --> 00:08:38,209
reopened as an Italian
restaurant in December 2011,

133
00:08:38,289 --> 00:08:40,725
and closed again in 2016.

134
00:08:40,725 --> 00:08:44,127
Since 2019, the mansion
has been run by the

135
00:08:44,207 --> 00:08:48,339
restaurant group 1-Group,
with a Spanish restaurant,

136
00:08:48,419 --> 00:08:52,225
a bar called the 1918
Heritage Bar, and a café.

137
00:08:52,225 --> 00:08:55,725
It is also a popular place for weddings.

138
00:08:55,725 --> 00:08:58,900
The family name is still on the map.

139
00:08:58,900 --> 00:09:03,168
Besides the mansion and the
two mosques, a new Alkaff

140
00:09:03,248 --> 00:09:07,925
Lake opened in Bidadari Park
on the 3rd of September 2024,

141
00:09:08,005 --> 00:09:12,025
about 500 metres north of
where the old lake was.

142
00:09:12,025 --> 00:09:16,356
It is 1.8 hectares in size
and doubles as a flood-control

143
00:09:16,436 --> 00:09:19,529
pond for the new Bidadari housing estate,

144
00:09:19,609 --> 00:09:24,175
whose newest neighbourhood is
also named after the Alkaffs.

145
00:09:24,175 --> 00:09:28,050
Where it fits in the bigger
story: The Alkaffs were one of

146
00:09:28,130 --> 00:09:32,959
a small number of Hadhrami families
who helped build colonial Singapore,

147
00:09:33,039 --> 00:09:35,345
and whose money also built schools,

148
00:09:35,425 --> 00:09:39,925
roads and palaces in a desert
valley thousands of kilometres away.

149
00:09:39,925 --> 00:09:44,507
Most of their landmarks have gone,
but their name has outlived them,

150
00:09:44,587 --> 00:09:47,318
on two mosques, a new lake and a house on

151
00:09:47,398 --> 00:09:51,100
a hill that most visitors
know only as a place to eat.
